<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Madonna of Częstochowa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Madonna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a> is an icon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> and Child kept at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery">Jasna Góra Monastery</a> in Częstochowa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Brought there in 1382 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław II of Opole</a>, it bears two scars on the Virgin's cheek, traditionally attributed to Hussite raiders in 1430. Credited with saving the monastery during the Swedish siege of 1655, it became Poland's most revered religious image.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-madonna-of-czestochowa.jpg" alt="Black Madonna of Częstochowa" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrzej Otrębski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Czestochowa_MB_Czestochowska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
